{% extends 'layout.twig' %}

{% macro shot(file, w, h, alt, caption, eager) %}
  <figure class="home-shot">
    <picture>
      {# 640/960/1200/1600w WebP; sizes = hero/feature media column (7fr of the 1120px wrap on desktop). #}
      <source type="image/webp"
        srcset="{{ asset('home/' ~ file ~ '-640.webp') }} 640w, {{ asset('home/' ~ file ~ '-960.webp') }} 960w, {{ asset('home/' ~ file ~ '-1200.webp') }} 1200w, {{ asset('home/' ~ file ~ '.webp') }} 1600w"
        sizes="(min-width: 1180px) 626px, (min-width: 900px) 55vw, calc(100vw - 2.5rem)">
      <img src="{{ asset('home/' ~ file ~ '.png') }}" width="{{ w }}" height="{{ h }}" alt="{{ alt }}"{% if not eager %} loading="lazy"{% endif %} decoding="async">
    </picture>
    {% if caption %}<figcaption>{{ caption }}</figcaption>{% endif %}
  </figure>
{% endmacro %}

{% block body %}
{% if authed %}
  <p class="slow-fetch-warning muted">Some tools take a moment — we fetch fresh data from OSM.</p>
  <div class="card home-signed-in">
    <h2>{% if groupName %}{{ groupName }}{% else %}Your group{% endif %}</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Signed in{% if fullName %} as <strong>{{ fullName }}</strong>{% endif %}.
      Tools use your OSM account. Most fetch from OSM when you open them. Members and Group numbers wait until you choose sections and load.
    </p>
    {% include '_rate_limit.twig' %}
    <div class="row">
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/help/">Help</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/logout/">Logout</a>
    </div>
  </div>
  {% if osmRecentErrors %}
  <div class="card">
    <details>
      <summary class="muted">Recent OSM API errors, all groups ({{ osmRecentErrors|length }})</summary>
      <p class="muted">Admin only (ADMIN_OSM_USER_IDS). Status codes only, no OSM message text or member details (invalid-action, 403, 429, transport). Full file: <code>storage/osm-errors.jsonl</code> on the server.</p>
      <ul class="muted" style="font-size:0.9rem">
      {% for e in osmRecentErrors|reverse %}
        <li><code>{{ e.at|default('—') }}</code>
          · {{ e.kind|default('http') }}
          · {{ e.method|default('') }} {{ e.endpoint|default('') }}
          {% if e.action %} · action={{ e.action }}{% endif %}
          {% if e.http_status %} · HTTP {{ e.http_status }}{% endif %}
          {% if e.osm_code %} · code {{ e.osm_code }}{% endif %}
          {% if e.section_id %} · section {{ e.section_id }}{% endif %}
          {% if e.badge_id %} · badge {{ e.badge_id }}{% endif %}
        </li>
      {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </details>
  </div>
  {% endif %}
{% else %}
{% import _self as home %}
<div class="home-public">

  <section class="home-hero" aria-labelledby="home-hero-h">
    <div class="home-hero-text">
      <h2 id="home-hero-h">Waiting list, badges and kit, sorted from your OSM data</h2>
      <p class="home-subline">OSMHelper signs in to Online Scout Manager with your own login and puts the jobs that take ages in OSM onto one page each: agreeing who gets the next place, working out top awards, counting nights away, and tidying equipment. It can also put a joining form on your WordPress website that adds children straight to your OSM waiting list.</p>
      <p class="home-actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/auth/">Connect with OSM</a>
        <a class="btn-link" href="/help/">Read the guide first</a>
      </p>
      <p class="home-oauth-note">You'll be sent to OSM to give permission. OSMHelper keeps your OSM access token in the server session only, unless you turn on the WordPress joining form. Most tools then read fresh data from OSM. The member list and group numbers wait until you choose sections.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="home-hero-media">
      {{ home.shot('waiting-list', 1600, 880, "OSMHelper waiting list showing fictional applicants with age, days waiting, suggested section and a score, with one score's working shown: age 10.9 times 3 equals 32.7, plus 29.2 months, plus 20 for willing to help, equals 81.9.", 'The waiting list, with the working behind each score. Made-up names.', true) }}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-note" aria-labelledby="home-note-h">
    <h3 id="home-note-h">Why I built this</h3>
    {# Final wording from Jason, 28 Sep. #}
    <p>I'm the Group Lead Volunteer at 4th Ashby Scout Group in Leicestershire. OSM holds everything we need, but some jobs mean clicking through screen after screen: deciding who's next to join from our waiting list, checking who's close to their Chief Scout's Award, or counting nights away for a young person who has moved up a section.</p>
    <p>So I built OSMHelper to fill those gaps. It pulls our group's own data out of OSM and lays it out for the job in hand. When you change member details, such as waiting list ranks and notes, they're saved back into OSM, so OSM stays the one record of your members. Other groups can use it too.</p>
    <p>It's a volunteer project. If something is wrong or missing, email me at <a href="mailto:hello@jonbloor.co.uk">hello@jonbloor.co.uk</a>.</p>
    <p class="home-signature">Jon Bloor<br>Group Lead Volunteer, 4th Ashby Scout Group</p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-feature" aria-labelledby="home-f-wl">
    <div class="home-feature-text">
      <h3 id="home-f-wl">Agree the waiting list order, and keep it in OSM</h3>
      <p>OSMHelper reads your waiting list from OSM and suggests a section for each applicant from their age and your own age cut-offs. It gives each one a simple score: age times three, plus months on the list, plus 20 if the family is willing to help. Tap a score to see how it was worked out.</p>
      <p>Leaders can then set their own rank (1 is first to be offered a place), add a dated note and record whether a family is willing to help. Before anything is saved, you see exactly what will change. It's all saved to custom fields on each applicant's record in OSM. The score itself is never saved.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Works with more than one waiting list</li>
        <li>Earlier notes are kept, and each new note is stamped with the date and your name</li>
        <li>If another leader changed something since you opened the page, that row isn't overwritten unless you choose to</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="home-feature-media">
      {{ home.shot('waiting-review', 1600, 651, 'Review changes screen listing, for three fictional applicants, the value in OSM now and the new rank, willing-to-help and dated note that will be saved.', 'Review exactly what will change before anything is written to OSM.') }}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-feature home-feature--flip" aria-labelledby="home-f-gn">
    <div class="home-feature-text">
      <h3 id="home-f-gn">See where you have spaces</h3>
      <p>One page shows members, leaders, capacity and spaces for each section, with how many are waiting and at what age. Tick the sections from your OSM sign-in, then load. OSMHelper fetches those sections and the waiting list, and leaves out Explorers, Adults, and any section switched off in Settings. Charts show members against spaces, the waiting list by age band, and how full each section is. You set section capacity and age cut-offs in Settings.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="home-feature-media">
      {{ home.shot('group-numbers', 1600, 571, 'Group numbers page with bar charts of members and spaces for each section and a chart of waiting-list children by age band.', 'Group numbers, as of page load.') }}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-feature home-feature--text" aria-labelledby="home-f-wp">
    <div class="home-feature-text">
      <h3 id="home-f-wp">A joining form on your WordPress website</h3>
      <p>Parents fill in a waiting-list form on your group's own website, and OSMHelper adds the child straight to your OSM waiting list using your OSM login. Parents don't need an OSM login, and your group doesn't need to set up its own OSM app or API keys. You install a WordPress plugin, paste in a site key, and add the form to a page.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Child's details, home address and up to two parents, saved as Primary Contacts in OSM</li>
        <li>Each parent can choose to receive text messages from leaders</li>
        <li>An optional note from the parent, added to your waiting list notes with the date</li>
        <li>A confirmation email to parents, address lookup by postcode or Google, and optional captcha against spam</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-feature" aria-labelledby="home-f-badges">
    <div class="home-feature-text">
      <h3 id="home-f-badges">Top awards and nights away without the spreadsheet</h3>
      <p><strong>Top awards:</strong> pick a section and OSMHelper works out each member's progress towards the Chief Scout's Bronze, Silver or Gold award. You review what's in OSM now against what it suggests, export a copy, then choose which rows to save back to OSM.</p>
      <p><strong>Nights away:</strong> pick one or more young people to see their camps and nights from the Nights Away staged badge in OSM, including camps recorded in a previous section before they moved up. Export it to PDF with their name and totals.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="home-feature-media">
      {{ home.shot('top-awards', 1600, 610, "Top awards page comparing current and proposed Chief Scout's award progress for fictional Scouts, with tick boxes to choose which to save.", 'Top awards: check before you save.') }}
      {{ home.shot('nights-away', 1600, 1349, 'Nights away table listing camps and nights for two fictional members, with totals.', '') }}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-feature home-feature--flip" aria-labelledby="home-f-kit">
    <div class="home-feature-text">
      <h3 id="home-f-kit">Kit and bank accounts in tables you can search</h3>
      <p><strong>Equipment:</strong> all your quartermaster lists in one table you can sort, search and export, with broken items highlighted. Tick several items and move them to a location from your own list. OSMHelper updates the Location in OSM after you confirm. You can also add an item: fill in the same fields OSM uses, review them, then confirm. Editing an existing item is still to come.</p>
      <p><strong>Finance:</strong> bank account balances and transactions, plus every transfer across your active accounts with any unmatched in or out leg highlighted, which helps when the balances don't add up. Finance is read-only: OSMHelper never changes your accounts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="home-feature-media">
      {{ home.shot('equipment', 1600, 608, 'Equipment table with three tents ticked and the scout hut loft chosen as the location to move them to.', 'Move kit in bulk. You confirm before OSM is updated.') }}
      {{ home.shot('all-transfers', 1600, 824, 'All transfers table with one unmatched transfer highlighted. Amounts and account names are made up.', '') }}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-also" aria-labelledby="home-also-h">
    <h3 id="home-also-h">Also included</h3>
    <dl>
      <div><dt>Members:</dt> <dd>tick the sections from your OSM sign-in, then load youth members and young leaders, with ages filled in from OSM.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Find a member:</dt> <dd>search by postcode, phone number or parent name to see which child a call or form is about, and which section they’re in.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Events:</dt> <dd>every event in a section by start date, for a date range or all time, with archived events marked. Download the list as a CSV.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Checks:</dt> <dd>young people in more than one section, plus Young Leader and leader checks, marked OK or needing a look.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Joining form:</dt> <dd>a waiting-list form for your WordPress website that adds children to OSM. See above.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Settings:</dt> <dd>age cut-offs, section capacity, which sections hold your kit and your accounts, and your list of kit locations.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tables:</dt> <dd>most tables can be sorted, searched and exported as CSV, Excel or PDF.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="home-before" aria-labelledby="home-before-h">
    <h3 id="home-before-h">Before you connect</h3>
    <div class="home-before-cols">
      <div>
        <h4>You'll need</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>An Online Scout Manager login with access to the sections you want to see. OSMHelper can only show what your OSM login can see.</li>
          <li>To say yes when OSM asks for permission. Saving ranks, notes, awards or kit locations needs permission to update records, so you may be asked to sign in again the first time.</li>
          <li>A bit of patience. Most pages fetch fresh from OSM, so some take a moment, and each fetch uses part of your OSM API allowance. OSMHelper shows how much you have left. The member list and group numbers only fetch the sections you tick. Group numbers also reads the waiting list then.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>It doesn't</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Replace OSM. OSM stays the record, and anything OSMHelper saves goes into OSM.</li>
          {# Privacy wording (privacy-hardening). Each claim is backed by code:
             sessions end 2 hours after sign-in even while in use (Config::sessionAbsoluteSeconds(), enforced in
             App::enforceSessionMaxAge(); logout calls session_destroy()); session files live in storage/sessions/
             with gc_maxlifetime 7200, so PHP's session GC deletes them shortly after;
             Top awards caches are deleted after 2 hours and at logout (TopAwardsController::sweepExpiredCaches);
             the OSM read cache (storage/osm-cache, OsmCache: waiting-list applicants' names, dates of birth, dates
             joined, every waiting-list custom field incl. leaders' notes, plus the section list) is keyed to the
             OSM user who fetched it and only read back for them (OsmCache::get/put), each entry is deleted 5 minutes
             after it was fetched by OsmCache::sweep() on every request (App::sweepPrivateCaches), at sign-out
             (OsmCache::forgetUser) and, for a waiting list, when OSMHelper writes to it (WaitingListService::invalidateCache);
             Young Leaders uses the same cache (kinds ylsection / ylmember, OsmCache::KIND_TTLS = 24h) holding only
             first/last name, age text and the names of other sections (YoungLeaders::slimMember), owner-only,
             deleted 24h after fetch by the same sweep and at sign-out (forgetUser); an expired session only clears it on that
             browser's next request (App::enforceSessionMaxAge) and a new sign-in clears only the previous user in that
             browser (App::beginAuthenticatedSession); Young Leaders limits (storage/yl-limits, YoungLeaderLimits:
             timestamps, section ids and counts, keyed-hash file names, 24h) and OSM rate state (storage/osm-rate:
             numbers and reason codes) are kept across sign-out on purpose;
             OSM user ids are stored with settings (waiting_field_maps.updated_by, wordpress_site_keys.osm_user_id);
             osm-errors.jsonl never stores OSM message text or member IDs and is pruned after 30 days on every
             request (deploy runs bin/scrub-error-log.php for older lines); the WordPress form's OSM tokens are
             encrypted (TokenCrypto; deploy runs bin/encrypt-site-tokens.php, and production refuses to save
             them unencrypted). #}
          <li>Keep copies of member data. Your OSM access token is held only in your server session. Anything a page holds for you while you work (a member list, group numbers, events, or the contact details Find a member searches) stays in that session only. To save OSM requests, the waiting list pages keep a copy on our server for 5 minutes: applicants’ names, dates of birth, dates joined, all your waiting list custom fields and the leaders’ notes, plus your section list. Only you can see that copy, and it is deleted 5 minutes after it was fetched, when you sign out, or when OSMHelper saves a change to that waiting list. Young Leaders keeps a copy of members’ names, ages and the names of the other sections they are in, for up to 24 hours, so going back to it doesn’t use your OSM allowance. Only you can see that copy, and it is deleted 24 hours after it was fetched or when you sign out. Every session ends 2 hours after you sign in, or when you sign out, and its file on our server is cleaned up shortly after. Find a member searches, and the members you pick in Nights away, are sent privately, so they are not saved in web addresses or server logs. Top awards results are kept for up to 2 hours so the page loads faster, then deleted. The only things saved on our server are your group settings (age cut-offs, capacity, which sections each tool uses, kit locations and your chosen waiting list fields), saved with your OSM user number so we know whose settings are whose, and, if you turn on the WordPress joining form, an encrypted OSM access key for that form. To protect your OSM allowance we also keep a few times and counts, with no names: when you last loaded Young Leaders or pressed Refresh, the size of a section too big for it (these are kept for 24 hours, even after you sign out, so signing in again doesn’t skip the waits), and how often OSM has been asked lately, so OSMHelper can slow down or pause if OSM asks it to. We also keep a short error log to fix problems. It records what failed, the error code and which section, never OSM’s message text, names, contact details or member numbers, and entries are deleted after 30 days.</li>
          <li>Keep the forms parents send. If you use the WordPress joining form, each form is passed straight to OSM and not kept. To do that, OSMHelper keeps an encrypted OSM access key for the form (not your password) on the server until you turn the form off.</li>
          <li>Change your bank accounts. Finance pages are read-only.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="home-independent">This is an independent project, not affiliated with Online Scout Manager or The Scout Association. It was built for one Scout group's needs, so it might not suit yours.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-faq" aria-labelledby="home-faq-h">
    <h3 id="home-faq-h">Questions</h3>
    <details>
      <summary>How is the waiting list score worked out?</summary>
      <p>Age in years (8 years 6 months counts as 8.5) times three, plus months on the list, plus 20 if they're willing to help. It's a starting point. Your leaders' rank is what decides, for example to keep siblings together. The full explanation is in the <a href="/help/">Help</a> guide.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Will it change anything in OSM without asking?</summary>
      <p>No. Waiting list ranks and notes, top awards, equipment moves and new equipment items all show you what will change first. Nothing is saved until you confirm.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can parents join our waiting list from our website?</summary>
      <p>Yes, if your website uses WordPress. Install the OSMHelper joining form plugin, choose your OSM waiting list in OSMHelper, and add the form to a page. Each form goes straight into your OSM waiting list. The <a href="/help/#wordpress-form">Help</a> guide explains the steps.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Does it cost anything?</summary>
      <p>No. It's free to use.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What's coming next?</summary>
      <p>Editing equipment items, and uploading badges from a spreadsheet, are planned. See the <a href="/roadmap/">Roadmap</a>, and the <a href="/roadmap/#history">Changelog</a> for what's changed recently.</p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="home-closing" aria-label="Get started">
    <p class="home-closing-line">Ready to try it with your own OSM login?</p>
    <p><a class="btn" href="/auth/">Connect with OSM</a></p>
    <p class="home-links">Useful for leaders: <a href="https://wogglehq.com" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Woggle HQ</a> · Source code: <a href="https://github.com/jonbloor/OSMHelper" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub</a></p>
  </section>

</div>
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
